{"body":{"text":["Yeah, let's think about the requirements and situations a bit. First of all, if you something like, well, we certainly know at the root we want to be able to, well, that's part of what OpenAI did, allowing certain things to happen or making sure that you catch them at the root first, right? Not that. Now it makes sense for some kind of propagation, but it also makes sense to be able to, at the root, set the rules of the game, right? But then it also makes sense that clicking something in a box propagates, but maybe it's never that you stop the bubbling. I don't know yet exactly what we're building, so we're going to continue to explore here. But using up and down arrow keys, etc., for selection navigation, I mean, maybe that's not something that you want anyone to have to implement themselves like that, right? That's a kind of standard thing. But it makes sense that in a reader, for instance, if I press something, it's given a certain Chrome. So if we explore the reader from OpenLight, it was using collations, and you'd be able to select something and then select something else, and they maybe have different colors as their different collation selections. It's you're welcome to read about that case where we're exploring agent sessions and how you may navigate that. And so then, of course, pressing something is something that the reader handles. But there's also a certain amount of Chrome, and that's something that we haven't discussed either. Having a kind of framework for Chrome around nested components, or nested components can't be that, and the Chrome resides within the list itself. But then that's also something, that's also an area to look into. If it is the parent then that does the Chrome, or the child that does the Chrome, and if the child does it, then it can't be decoupled, then it has to hold the kind of Chrome. And if the parent does it, then the parent might be the one that is responsible for whether or not it's a card, for its kind of surface of something, right? And then the child doesn't care for the surface. So then cards is something that the child never creates, the parents always creates the card. So that's a little something that we need to explore regardless. But we may take inspiration from the collations and the readers as we consider. So that is the case. Then another thing that we're going to have to explore here as well, and create with these to some degree of priority, is overlays. So currently, the chassis is already a nesting kind of thing. But if we look at OpenAI's drawings, you'd actually be able to mount different paradigms that then take the holarchy and work appropriately. So the other alternative is having then 2 layers. You have that layer, then you have an overlay layer. If you want to do a kiosk mode, then maybe you do something differently. And the whole idea about being able to have any kinds of layers you want with any type of implementation is interesting. So I know that we're soon enough definitely going to want to have node-based layouts as well. So to be able to look upon the holarchy of the UI as a node layout is really powerful, I think. Overall, but especially holarchies, and naturally the holarchies of your interface. And that is something that was explored in OpenLight, where you can actually nest them, and there's kind of, it was an interesting, agnostic, quite powerful thing there. So we want to consider here, we don't want to bring in things prematurely, we don't want to convolute things without true grounding, so we might not adopt all of that, or we may. And yeah, the overlay thing is naturally important. We already have an expression input, right? But an expression input, it has its menu for auto-completion, right? So that should be rendered in an overlay. And if we right-click on something, that's also an overlay. So we also do that properly. And I also soon enough expect to see, I'm giving here various ideas that to certain degrees pertain to what we're talking about. Regardless, we're going to need to be able to persist these properly with elevation. So another thing is that I'm expecting that when we right-click, we'll have some helpful the way we compose the right-click is something that's also discussed a bit in OpenLight, and we haven't really covered. The way it's also, type matching in a way. So, but then there may also be completely global actions. So the action to be able to see the layout in a node view. I mean, I think that's a really powerful one. For anyone looking at this website, that's just a nice thing to do. It showcases what we can do with the system, and anyway, I think it's helpful. Now we might be prioritizing the Git integration there before some of these things, but I expect that when we persist these separate ideas as elevated, but they also tie in, some of them really tie into each other and to this discussion here. I guess accessibility in terms of focus is something that's already built in to web, but isn't necessarily built into all UI chassis and methods. If you do something like node layout, then perhaps it doesn't at all. So very interesting what we're going to explore here."]},"id":"1M88X8DXK610020","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
