{"body":{"text":["I don't know, you had a very narrow cases of ideas, and then you're sending away sub-agents on that particular thing. I don't know to what degree they are informed, how much of the knowledge, etc., and all they read. I'm not particularly trusting this process. The input's own popover rather than a record in the overlay layer, well, that's the absolute opposite of what's been drawn. So that would have to be really grounded further and taken apart. That reasoning. I don't get this idea about stops above what was pressed. The inspector should read DOM attributes to get around this. I really don't get this. I get a bad feeling. This really has to be taken further and broken apart in terms of its reasoning. And it's already having an idea about Chrome. I don't trust it either. But a focus ring is a type of Chrome as well. So it goes to show that it might need to be some kind of framework way of being able to splice into the holarchy and do something. But again, I don't see how it could be intelligent enough to know the shape of what it is. Because if you're selecting, is it always a rounded outline? How does that work with everything? I'm not sure yet. And different themes then still would have different. So it's tough for me because it's a very frameworky thing. It's not I don't get that yet. I don't know if ARIA roles are enough, if that works with all kinds of layouts, or if we need some other data that is perhaps simpler but more powerful. I'm skeptic. Yeah, of course, pressing a button is pressing a button. Nothing's to be propagated there, I think, because then you're deliberately catching it. But I can't say for sure that that's a golden rule. That's me reading the messages from each agent, but then I'm faced with a wall of text. And I've already given most of my critique. So no, I'm trusting almost nothing here. And this wasn't a particularly intelligent way of running agents either."]},"id":"1M88X8DXK610028","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
